==Macedonian==

===Etymology===
From Serbian Cyrillic letter [[џ#Serbian|џ]] (''dž''), adopted in 1945 by the Central Committee of [[w:League of Communists of Yugoslavia|the Communist Party of Yugoslavia]].

===Letter===
{{head|mk|letter|lower case||upper case|Џ|italic|[[џ|''џ'']]|upper case italic|[[Џ|''Џ'']]}}

# The thirtieth letter of the Macedonian [[Cyrillic]] alphabet. Its name is {{term|џ|tr=dž|lang=mk}} and it has a similar sound to the English '''dg''' in ''do'''dg'''e''. It is preceded by [[ч|Ч]] and followed by [[ш|Ш]].

----

==Serbo-Croatian==

===Alternative forms===
* {{l|sh|dž|sc=Latn}} (Serbo-Croatian Latin/Roman alphabet)

===Etymology 1===
From [[џ#Translingual|џ]] of the 15th century [[w:Romanian Cyrillic alphabet|Romanian Cyrillic alphabet]], first used by Serbian scribes in the 17th century and part of [[w:Vuk Stefanović Karadžić|Vuk Stefanović Karadžić’s]] Cyrillic alphabet reform.

====Pronunciation====
* {{qualifier|phoneme}} {{IPA|/dʒ/|lang=sh}}

====Letter====
{{head|sh|letter|lower case||upper case|Џ}}

# The twenty-ninth letter of the Serbo-Croatian [[Cyrillic]] alphabet. It is preceded by the letter {{l|sh|ч|sc=Cyrl}} (''č'') and is followed by the letter {{l|sh|ш|sc=Cyrl}} (''š'').

=====Usage notes=====
Its name is {{term|џ|tr=dž|lang=sh}} and it has a similar sound to the English '''dg''' in ''do'''dg'''e''.

===Etymology 2===
A shortening of {{term|џабе|lang=sh|sc=Cyrl}}.

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|/dʒ/|lang=sh}}

====Adverb====
{{sh-adverb|head=џ|c|dž}}

# {{slang|lang=sh}} [[for free]]; very [[cheap]]

